INT. FISH MARKET AUCTION FLOOR - EARLY MORNING

Crushed ice. Crates of silver mackerel. A scale. A bell. The din of a hundred voices that are not in this scene.

MAUDE PETTIGREW, 50s, galoshes and a straw hat with a feather in it, grips a number paddle. FERDIE "FERDINAND" LOMBARD, 50s, a cravat under his oilskins, grips his.

MAUDE
Ferdinand Lombard, put that down.

FERDIE
I will not, Maudie.

MAUDE
It's my tuna.

FERDIE
It's the market's tuna.

MAUDE
I saw it first.

FERDIE
You saw it second. I saw it from the harbor.

MAUDE
(to the crowd)
Did anyone else see a man claim a tuna from the harbor?

FERDIE
(to the crowd)
Did anyone else see a woman in a feather hat claim anything?

An invisible auctioneer raps a gavel. Both snap their paddles up.

MAUDE
Two hundred!

FERDIE
Two hundred!

MAUDE
Two-fifty!

FERDIE
Two-fifty!

MAUDE
You are impossible!

FERDIE
You are impossible!

The gavel bangs. They speak at the same instant.

MAUDE
I'm bidding for my sister's wedding.

^FERDIE
I'm bidding for my sister's wedding.

They stop. Stare at each other. Slowly lower their paddles.

MAUDE
Your sister's Cordelia?

FERDIE
Your sister's Cordelia?

MAUDE
She's marrying a Lombard.

FERDIE
She's marrying a Pettigrew.

MAUDE
Then we're...

FERDIE
Cousins-in-law.

MAUDE
(to herself)
Oh, dear.

FERDIE
Split the tuna?

MAUDE
Split the tuna.

They shake hands. The gavel falls. SOLD, says the unseen auctioneer, to both.
